\begin{abstract}
    % For Open Review
    % Asynchronous Stochastic Gradient Descent (ASGD) is widely used to parallelize the learning process in distributed machine learning tasks, but its “wild” nature—leveraging every available computational resource—results in significant delays in gradient updates. 
    % This issue is exacerbated when computation times vary arbitrarily across workers and becomes even more pronounced as the number of workers increases.
    % While many ASGD variants have been proposed to address these delays, recent studies by Tyurin and Richtarik (NeurIPS 2024) show that their time complexity remains suboptimal.
    % Moreover, their proposed minimax optimal method, Rennala SGD, is synchronous.
    % In this paper, we introduce Ringmaster ASGD, a method designed to tame the “wild” nature of ASGD. 
    % Our theoretical analysis demonstrates that Ringmaster ASGD achieves optimal time complexity under arbitrarily heterogeneous computation times, making it the first ASGD method to be optimal. 
    % We also extend our method to handle dynamically changing computation times, with the generalized version maintaining optimal time complexity. 
    % Finally, numerical experiments show that Ringmaster ASGD outperforms the previous optimal method, Rennala SGD, in practice.

    % Asynchronous Stochastic Gradient Descent (\algname{Asynchronous SGD}) is widely used to parallelize the learning process in distributed machine learning tasks. 
    % % but its “wild” nature—leveraging every available computational resource—results in significant delays in gradient updates. 
    % This issue is exacerbated when computation times vary arbitrarily across workers and becomes even more pronounced as the number of workers increases.
    % While many \algname{Asynchronous SGD} variants have been proposed to address these delays, recent studies by \citet{tyurin2024optimal} show that their time complexity remains suboptimal. 
    % % Moreover, their proposed minimax optimal method, \algname{Rennala SGD}, is \textit{synchronous}. 
    % In this paper, we introduce \algname{Ringmaster ASGD}, a method designed to tame the “wild” nature of \algname{Asynchronous SGD}. 
    % Our theoretical analysis demonstrates that \algname{Ringmaster ASGD} achieves optimal time complexity under arbitrarily heterogeneous computation times, making it the first \algname{Asynchronous SGD} method to be optimal. 
    % We also extend our method to handle dynamically changing computation times, with the generalized version maintaining optimal time complexity. 
    % Finally, numerical experiments show that \algname{Ringmaster ASGD} outperforms the previous optimal method, \algname{Rennala SGD}, in practice.

    Asynchronous Stochastic Gradient Descent (\algname{Asynchronous SGD}) is a cornerstone method for parallelizing learning in distributed machine learning. 
    However, its performance suffers under arbitrarily heterogeneous computation times across workers, leading to suboptimal time complexity and inefficiency as the number of workers scales. 
    While several \algname{Asynchronous SGD} variants have been proposed, recent findings by \citet{tyurin2024optimal} reveal that none achieve optimal time complexity, leaving a significant gap in the literature. 
    In this paper, we propose \algname{Ringmaster ASGD}, a novel \algname{Asynchronous SGD} method designed to address these limitations and tame the inherent challenges of \algname{Asynchronous SGD}. 
    We establish, through rigorous theoretical analysis, that \algname{Ringmaster ASGD} achieves optimal time complexity under arbitrarily heterogeneous and dynamically fluctuating worker computation times. 
    This makes it the first \algname{Asynchronous SGD} method to meet the theoretical lower bounds for time complexity in such scenarios.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

We consider stochastic nonconvex optimization problems of the form
\begin{equation*}
  \min_{x \in \R^d} \left\{f(x) \eqdef \ExpSub{\xi \sim \cD}{f(x;\xi)} \right\},
\end{equation*}
where $f \,:\, \R^d \times \mathbb{S}_{\xi} \to \R,$ $\R^d$ is a linear space, and $\mathbb{S}_{\xi}$ is a sample space.
In machine learning, $f(x;\xi)$ denotes the loss of a model parameterized by $x$ on a data sample $\xi$, and $\cD$ denotes the distribution of the training dataset.
In nonconvex optimization, our goal is to find an $\varepsilon$--stationary point, i.e., a (random) vector $\bar{x} \in \R^d$ such that $\mathbb{E}\left[\norm{\nabla f(\bar{x})}^2\right] \leq \varepsilon$.

We consider a setup involving $n$ workers (e.g., CPUs, GPUs, servers), each with access to the same distribution $\cD$.
Each worker is capable of computing independent, unbiased stochastic gradients with bounded variance (Assumption \ref{ass:stochastic_variance_bounded}).
We consider a setup with asynchronous, heterogeneous, and varying computation speeds.
We aim to account for all potential scenarios, such as random outages, varying computational performance over time, and the presence of slow or straggling workers \citep{dean2013tail}.

This setup is common in both datacenter environments \citep{dean2012large} and federated learning \citep{konevcny2016federated,mcmahan2016federated,kairouz2021advances} for distributed training. 
Although parallelism facilitates rapid convergence, variations in worker speeds make effective coordination more challenging.

Asynchronous Stochastic Gradient Descent (\algname{Asynchronous SGD}) is a popular approach for parallelization in such distributed settings.
The algorithm operates as follows (\Cref{alg:asgd}), formalized below.
